window.KHAN_NOTES = [{"slug":"private-addressing","title":"Private IPv4 & NAT","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"A private IP identifies a device inside a network. NAT lets devices using private addresses communicate through an internet-facing address.","body":"\u003ch2>Start with the address\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An IPv4 address is four numbers separated by dots, such as \u003ccode>10.0.0.25\u003c/code>. Each number can be 0–255. Your laptop needs an address so other devices can send traffic to it. A \u003cstrong>private\u003c/strong> address can be reused inside different homes and businesses because it is not routed directly across the public internet.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Private range\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Example\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>10.0.0.0–10.255.255.255\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.0.0.25\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>172.16.0.0–172.31.255.255\u003c/td>\u003ctd>172.20.5.10\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>192.168.0.0–192.168.255.255\u003c/td>\u003ctd>192.168.1.20\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A 10.x address is just as private as a 192.168.x address. The administrator or router configuration chooses which range to use. Only 172.16 through 172.31 is private in the 172 range. Also notice the spelling: \u003ccode>196.168\u003c/code> is different from \u003ccode>192.168\u003c/code>. Addresses outside these ranges are not automatically public; other special ranges exist.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>What NAT actually changes\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Network Address Translation (NAT)\u003c/strong> changes addresses as traffic passes through a router or firewall. In a typical office, the device changes a laptop’s private source address into the address used on the internet side. \u003cstrong>Port Address Translation (PAT)\u003c/strong> also tracks port numbers, allowing many laptops to share one public address. A port identifies a particular service or connection on a device.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Laptop                  Office gateway              Website\n10.0.0.25:53010    →     203.0.113.10:62001     →     TCP 443\n                        Translation recorded\nReply to :62001   →     matches the record     →     laptop :53010\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>The gateway keeps a translation table so replies return to the right laptop. The public address above is reserved for documentation. Real connections can also pass through another translation at the internet provider.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Different networks can share an internet connection\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An office might put staff in \u003ccode>10.10.10.0/24\u003c/code> and guests in \u003ccode>192.168.20.0/24\u003c/code>. Both can use the same public internet address. Creating another VLAN or subnet does not automatically create or require another public IP. Internal separation and internet address translation are separate settings.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Use it on a support or security ticket\u003c/h2>\u003cp>If a user reports “my IP is 10.x instead of 192.168.x,” that alone is not a fault. Compare their address, subnet mask, gateway, and DNS servers with the intended network using \u003ccode>ipconfig /all\u003c/code>. When investigating an external service’s logs, its recorded source may be the office’s public address. Use the timestamp and translation or connection records to identify an internal device; do not assume that public address identifies one employee.\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>Remember\u003c/strong>\u003cp>NAT changes addresses. Firewall rules decide which traffic is allowed. A malicious program can still make outbound connections through NAT.\u003c/p>\u003c/aside>","question":"Do a staff VLAN and a guest VLAN need separate public IP addresses?","answer":"No. They can use different private subnets and share a public address through PAT. Their separation depends on VLAN configuration and the rules controlling traffic between them, not on having different public IPs.","sources":["ip","nat"],"related":["subnetting","vlans"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"RFC 1918 — Private address space","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918"},{"title":"RFC 3022 — Traditional NAT","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3022"}]},{"slug":"subnetting","title":"Subnetting","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Practiced calculations","principle":"Subnetting divides an IP address range into smaller networks. The subnet mask tells a device which addresses are local and which need a router.","body":"\u003ch2>What the slash means\u003c/h2>\u003cp>In \u003ccode>192.168.204.30/28\u003c/code>, the address identifies a device and \u003ccode>/28\u003c/code> describes its network size. IPv4 has 32 bits: 28 identify the network and the remaining 4 distinguish addresses inside it. A bit is one binary digit, 0 or 1. An \u003cstrong>octet\u003c/strong> is a group of eight bits: eight bits can represent 256 values, numbered 0–255.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>subnet mask\u003c/strong> writes those network bits as ones and the host bits as zeros. The /28 mask is \u003ccode>11111111.11111111.11111111.11110000\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>255.255.255.240\u003c/code>. For a destination on the same subnet, a host usually communicates directly. For another subnet, it normally sends the packet to its default gateway, a router interface.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Count the addresses\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"formula\">Host bits = 32 − prefix\u003cbr>Total addresses = 2\u003csup>host bits\u003c/sup>\u003cbr>Ordinary usable addresses = total − 2\u003c/div>\u003cp>For /28: 4 host bits give 16 total addresses. The first is the \u003cstrong>network address\u003c/strong>, the label for the whole subnet. The last is the \u003cstrong>broadcast address\u003c/strong>, used to address the subnet’s devices collectively. That leaves 14 ordinary host addresses. A gateway uses one of those 14; it is not a bonus address.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Work through 192.168.204.30/28\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>Find the mask: \u003ccode>255.255.255.240\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Find the increment in the changing octet: \u003ccode>256 − 240 = 16\u003c/code>. Fourth-octet blocks start at 0, 16, 32, 48, and so on.\u003c/li>\u003cli>30 sits in the 16–31 block. Network: \u003ccode>192.168.204.16\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The next block starts at .32, so broadcast is \u003ccode>192.168.204.31\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Usable addresses: \u003ccode>192.168.204.17–192.168.204.30\u003c/code>. The original address .30 is the last usable host.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"subnet\" aria-label=\"Move the network boundary\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>Move the network boundary\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Change the prefix or enter an address. Predict the network and broadcast before revealing the calculation.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>Enable JavaScript to use this exercise. The worked examples in this note cover the same ideas.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>When the boundary is in the third octet\u003c/h2>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>10.50.132.75/21\u003c/code>, 11 host bits give 2,048 total addresses. The mask is \u003ccode>255.255.248.0\u003c/code>. Its third-octet increment is \u003ccode>256 − 248 = 8\u003c/code>. The block containing 132 runs from 128 through 135.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Result\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Address\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Network\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.50.128.0\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Broadcast\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.50.135.255\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Usable range\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.50.128.1–10.50.135.254\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Ordinary capacity\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2,046 host addresses\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The increment is 8 in the third octet, while the entire subnet contains 2,048 addresses. These are different quantities. Calculate the broadcast from the network boundary, not by adding the increment to the original host address.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Use it at work\u003c/h2>\u003cp>For a department with 50 devices, /26 provides 62 ordinary host addresses, leaving room for a gateway and some growth. Subnetting also helps diagnose a wrong mask, an incorrect gateway, or an overly broad firewall rule. An address plan still needs gateways, DHCP settings, and switch configuration to become a working network.\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>Two exceptions\u003c/strong>\u003cp>The “minus two” rule describes ordinary IPv4 broadcast subnets. /31 point-to-point links can use both addresses; /32 identifies one address. Cloud platforms can reserve extra addresses.\u003c/p>\u003c/aside>","question":"Find the network, broadcast, mask, and ordinary usable capacity of 172.19.155.200/18.","answer":"The mask is 255.255.192.0. The increment is 64 in the third octet, so 155 sits in 128–191. Network: 172.19.128.0. Broadcast: 172.19.191.255. Usable range: 172.19.128.1–172.19.191.254, giving 16,382 addresses.","sources":["subnet","p31"],"related":["vlsm","dhcp"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Cisco — IPv4 addressing and subnetting","url":"https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/ip/routing-information-protocol-rip/13788-3.html"},{"title":"RFC 3021 — IPv4 /31 links","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3021"}]},{"slug":"vlsm","title":"VLSM","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Practiced calculations","principle":"Variable Length Subnet Masking gives each subnet a size that fits its needs, instead of giving every department the same-sized block.","body":"\u003ch2>Why use different sizes?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Suppose a warehouse needs 100 device addresses but a kiosk network needs only 12. Giving both a /25 would reserve 128 addresses for each. VLSM lets the warehouse use /25 and the kiosks use /28. Both subnets can fit inside a larger allocation without overlapping. Read \u003ca href=\"kb-subnetting.html\">Subnetting\u003c/a> first if network and broadcast addresses are still unfamiliar.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Choose a prefix from a requirement\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Choose enough host bits that \u003ccode>2^h − 2\u003c/code> covers the required host addresses. Then calculate \u003ccode>32 − h\u003c/code> for the prefix. For 100 devices, /26 gives only 62 usable addresses. /25 gives 126. Count the gateway, printers, management interfaces, and growth before finalizing a real plan.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Devices in this exercise\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Candidate\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Ordinary capacity\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>100\u003c/td>\u003ctd>/25\u003c/td>\u003ctd>126\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>50\u003c/td>\u003ctd>/26\u003c/td>\u003ctd>62\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>28\u003c/td>\u003ctd>/27\u003c/td>\u003ctd>30\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>12\u003c/td>\u003ctd>/28\u003c/td>\u003ctd>14\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch2>Allocate the biggest blocks first\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>10.60.24.0/24\u003c/code> as the parent allocation. Start with the largest requirement and place the next subnet after the previous subnet’s broadcast address. Each start must also be a valid boundary for its prefix.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Department\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Subnet\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Usable range\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Broadcast\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Warehouse\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.60.24.0/25\u003c/td>\u003ctd>.1–.126\u003c/td>\u003ctd>.127\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Engineering\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.60.24.128/26\u003c/td>\u003ctd>.129–.190\u003c/td>\u003ctd>.191\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sales\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.60.24.192/27\u003c/td>\u003ctd>.193–.222\u003c/td>\u003ctd>.223\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Kiosks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.60.24.224/28\u003c/td>\u003ctd>.225–.238\u003c/td>\u003ctd>.239\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>All shortened addresses keep the first three octets \u003ccode>10.60.24\u003c/code>. The final block, \u003ccode>10.60.24.240/28\u003c/code>, remains available. The first child can start at the same address as its parent: /24 describes the whole allocation, while /25 describes only its first half.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Catch the common mistakes\u003c/h2>\u003cul>\u003cli>A /26 starts at a multiple of 64 in the fourth octet: .0, .64, .128, or .192. “The next free address” is not enough if it is misaligned.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The network and broadcast are not ordinary device addresses. In the kiosk subnet, .224 is the network, .239 is the broadcast, and .225–.238 are usable.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The prefix is part of the network definition. Write \u003ccode>10.60.24.128/26\u003c/code>, not just “.128.”\u003c/li>\u003cli>Largest-first allocation is a practical method that avoids fragmentation. It is not a protocol requirement.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch2>Turn the plan into a working office\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Create the intended VLANs, assign their switch ports, and configure a router or Layer 3 switch interface for each subnet. Set up DHCP scopes with the matching mask, gateway, and DNS servers. Reserve or exclude statically assigned infrastructure addresses. Then apply rules for permitted traffic between departments and test both an allowed and a blocked connection. The calculation decides address capacity; these configuration steps implement it.\u003c/p>","question":"Why can’t 50 engineering devices use a /27, and what else should you count before choosing a /26?","answer":"A /27 has only 30 ordinary usable addresses. A /26 has 62. Count the gateway, other infrastructure, and expected growth as well as the 50 devices. Ensure the chosen block starts on a valid boundary and does not overlap another subnet.","sources":["subnet","p31"],"related":["subnetting","vlans"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Cisco — IPv4 addressing and subnetting","url":"https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/ip/routing-information-protocol-rip/13788-3.html"},{"title":"RFC 3021 — IPv4 /31 links","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3021"}]},{"slug":"vlans","title":"VLANs","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"A VLAN makes one physical switch behave like several separate local networks. Devices share a local broadcast network only when their ports belong to the same VLAN.","body":"\u003ch2>Picture an office switch\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A switch connects laptops, printers, and other devices by cable. Without separation, those devices may all share one local network. A \u003cstrong>Virtual Local Area Network\u003c/strong> assigns ports to separate groups. Staff can be in VLAN 10, guests in VLAN 20, and servers in VLAN 30, even when they connect to the same switch.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>broadcast\u003c/strong> is a message sent to every device in a local broadcast domain. For example, an ARP request asks which device owns a particular local IP. The switch floods that broadcast within its VLAN, not into every VLAN. This separation is the basic job of a VLAN.\u003c/p>\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"vlan\" aria-label=\"One switch. Separate networks.\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>One switch. Separate networks.\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Move the guest laptop between VLANs, then test its path to the server. Watch which boundary needs a routing policy.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>Enable JavaScript to use this exercise. The worked examples in this note cover the same ideas.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>Ports, tags, and subnets\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Term\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Meaning\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Access port\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Normally carries one VLAN for an end device. The attached laptop usually sends untagged frames.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Trunk port\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Carries multiple VLANs between network devices, commonly using 802.1Q tags to identify each VLAN.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Subnet\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The IP address range used on a network. A common office design assigns a different subnet to each VLAN.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Inter-VLAN routing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A router or Layer 3 switch moves traffic between VLANs. Firewall rules or access control lists can restrict that traffic.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A VLAN and a subnet are different settings. The VLAN is the local Ethernet separation; the subnet tells IP devices what is local. They are normally planned together. Giving a laptop an address from a different subnet does not move its switch port into another VLAN.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>What actually protects the server?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>If guests and servers are in different VLANs, a guest cannot directly reach the server through ordinary same-VLAN switching. But if a router connects the two networks and allows the traffic, the guest can still reach the server. The intended policy might allow guests to use the internet while denying connections to server addresses.\u003c/p>\u003cp>If an administrator accidentally places a guest port in the server VLAN, traffic may stay inside that VLAN and bypass the inter-VLAN firewall. Check VLAN membership as well as the rules between networks. Hosts and applications also need their own controls.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Use it on a client ticket\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>Confirm the device’s IP, mask, gateway, and physical switch port.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check the port’s VLAN and whether the expected VLAN is allowed on upstream trunks.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check that this VLAN has the right gateway and DHCP scope or relay.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For cross-VLAN access, inspect the route and the rule for the specific source, destination, protocol, and port.\u003c/li>\u003cli>After an approved change, verify the intended connection works and a forbidden connection remains blocked.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003caside class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>Public IPs are separate\u003c/strong>\u003cp>Staff and guest VLANs can share the same public IP for internet access. A VLAN creates local separation, not a new internet subscription.\u003c/p>\u003c/aside>","question":"A guest VLAN exists, but guests can still open the file server. What would you investigate?","answer":"Check that the guest is actually on the guest VLAN, then inspect routing and firewall or ACL rules between guests and servers. Separate VLANs alone do not guarantee a deny policy. Verify the exact service and both allowed and blocked paths.","sources":["vlan","subnet"],"related":["subnetting","ztna-sase"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Cisco — Inter-VLAN routing and access rules","url":"https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/smb/routers/cisco-rv-series-small-business-routers/1393-Inter-VLAN-Routing-with-Targeted-ACL-Restrictions.html"},{"title":"Cisco — IPv4 addressing and subnetting","url":"https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/ip/routing-information-protocol-rip/13788-3.html"}]},{"slug":"arp","title":"ARP","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"ARP finds the MAC address needed to deliver IPv4 traffic to the next device on a local network. For a remote destination, that device is usually your gateway.","keywords":"address resolution protocol MAC Ethernet broadcast cache next hop gateway spoofing poisoning","body":"\n\u003ch2>Why an IP address is not enough\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Your laptop wants to send data to a printer. It knows the printer’s IPv4 address, but an Ethernet network delivers local traffic using \u003cstrong>MAC addresses\u003c/strong>. A MAC address identifies a network interface on that link, often written as six hexadecimal pairs, such as \u003ccode>02:00:00:00:00:50\u003c/code>. It is different from the IP address and is not a permanent proof of device identity.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>IP packet\u003c/strong> carries the network-layer destination. For its trip across an Ethernet link, that packet sits inside a \u003cstrong>frame\u003c/strong> with local source and destination MAC addresses. \u003cstrong>Address Resolution Protocol (ARP)\u003c/strong> supplies the missing IPv4-to-MAC mapping for the next hop: the device that should receive this frame.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Ask the local network, then remember the reply\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Imagine a laptop at \u003ccode>10.20.10.25/24\u003c/code> and a printer at \u003ccode>10.20.10.50/24\u003c/code>, both on the same VLAN.\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\u003cli>The laptop checks its routing information. In this simple network, the printer is directly reachable on the local subnet.\u003c/li>\u003cli>It checks its \u003cstrong>ARP cache\u003c/strong>, a temporary table of IP-to-MAC mappings. A usable entry can avoid a new request.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Without an entry, it broadcasts an ARP request: “Who has 10.20.10.50? Tell 10.20.10.25.” The Ethernet destination is \u003ccode>ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff\u003c/code>, the broadcast address. The switch distributes it within that VLAN.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The printer normally replies directly to the laptop with its MAC address. The laptop caches the mapping and can address the frame to the printer.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Other devices can hear the request, but they do not normally answer for an address they do not own. ARP itself runs directly over the local link; it does not use a TCP or UDP port.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>What changes when the destination is remote?\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Now the laptop wants to reach a website outside its subnet. With a default route through \u003ccode>10.20.10.1\u003c/code>, it resolves \u003cstrong>the gateway’s MAC address\u003c/strong>. It does not broadcast across the internet asking for the website’s MAC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The frame is addressed to the gateway, while the packet inside still names the website’s IP as its destination. The router removes the incoming link-layer framing and forwards the packet using the next link’s addressing. Ordinary ARP broadcasts do not pass through routers. This is why understanding the subnet and gateway comes before interpreting an ARP lookup.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"arp\" aria-label=\"Choose the next hop\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>Who should answer?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Choose a printer or a remote website. Follow the lookup, request, reply, and first frame. Send again to see what the cache changes.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>Enable JavaScript to step through the exercise. For the local printer, resolve 10.20.10.50; for the remote website, resolve gateway 10.20.10.1. A cached mapping skips the initial broadcast.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2>Keep the different tables straight\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Mechanism\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Question it answers\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>DHCP\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Which IP configuration should this device use?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>DNS\u003c/td>\u003ctd>What address is associated with this name?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Routing table\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Which next hop and interface should carry this IP packet?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>ARP cache\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Which MAC address corresponds to that local IPv4 next hop?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Switch MAC table\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Which switch port leads toward this MAC address in this VLAN?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2>Use it on a connectivity ticket\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A laptop has a valid address but cannot reach the gateway. Check its address, mask, gateway, and VLAN first. On Windows, \u003ccode>arp -a\u003c/code> displays cached mappings, separated by interface. Compare the relevant next-hop entry with the expected device, and inspect a capture with Wireshark’s \u003ccode>arp\u003c/code> display filter if needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Repeated requests without replies can suggest an offline next hop, wrong VLAN, incorrect addressing, or a filtering/link problem. Check the capture location too. A MAC entry proves neither that the application works nor that its IP owner is authentic. Record evidence before clearing a cache; “delete everything and retry” can hide a useful clue.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Address changes and security\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Dynamic entries age or are refreshed according to the operating system. Devices can also announce an address mapping without first receiving an ordinary request, often called gratuitous ARP. ARP probes and announcements support address-conflict detection and legitimate changes such as failover, so a mapping change is not automatically an attack.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>ARP does not authenticate ownership of an address. False mappings, called \u003cstrong>ARP spoofing\u003c/strong> or \u003cstrong>cache poisoning\u003c/strong>, can redirect or disrupt local traffic. Investigate unexpected gateway mappings alongside device and switch evidence. On supported switches, Dynamic ARP Inspection validates ARP information against trusted bindings, commonly learned through DHCP snooping; static devices and trusted ports need appropriate configuration.\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>Scope to remember\u003c/strong>\u003cp>This note describes ordinary IPv4 over Ethernet. Proxy ARP is a special case where a router answers on another device’s behalf. IPv6 uses Neighbor Discovery rather than ARP.\u003c/p>\u003c/aside>","question":"Your laptop sends to a website outside its /24 subnet. Does ARP look up the website’s MAC address or the default gateway’s, and which IP remains inside the packet?","answer":"With the default route selected, ARP resolves the gateway’s local IPv4 address to its MAC. The Ethernet frame goes to that gateway, while the enclosed IP packet still names the website as its destination. A usable cached gateway mapping can avoid a new ARP request.","sources":["arp","arpConflict","arpWindows","arpInspection"],"related":["subnetting","vlans","dhcp","dns"],"references":[{"title":"RFC 826 — Address Resolution Protocol","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc826"},{"title":"RFC 5227 — IPv4 address conflict detection","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5227"},{"title":"Microsoft — View the ARP cache","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/arp"},{"title":"Cisco — Dynamic ARP Inspection","url":"https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/switches/lan-switch-software/222274-troubleshoot-dynamic-arp-inspection-dai.html"}]},{"slug":"dns","title":"DNS","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Scenario study","principle":"DNS translates names such as portal.example.com into information computers can use, often an IP address. It helps find a destination; it does not establish the connection.","body":"\u003ch2>Why names need a lookup\u003c/h2>\u003cp>People remember a name more easily than a server’s IP address. When an application needs an address, your computer checks available cached information and normally asks its configured \u003cstrong>DNS resolver\u003c/strong>. The resolver may answer from its cache or ask other DNS servers until it finds the authoritative answer. \u003cstrong>Authoritative\u003c/strong> means the server holds the published records for that DNS zone.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Caching saves repeated lookups. A record’s \u003cstrong>TTL\u003c/strong>, or time to live, tells a cache how long it may keep that record before checking again. This helps explain why a DNS change can appear to work on one device while another still uses an older answer.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Records you will encounter\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Record\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What it tells you\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>A / AAAA\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The IPv4 / IPv6 address associated with a name.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>CNAME\u003c/td>\u003ctd>An alias pointing to another name.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>MX\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Which mail servers receive email for a domain.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>TXT\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Text used for purposes such as domain verification and email authentication policies.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>PTR\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A reverse lookup from an IP address to a name, when configured.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>SRV\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Where a particular service can be found; Active Directory uses these to locate domain services.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch2>Separate a name problem from a service problem\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A user says websites fail, but your remote-support session still works. That proves some connectivity remains. It does not prove DNS is the cause: an existing session might not need a new lookup, and websites may use a different path or policy.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>ipconfig /all\nnslookup portal.example.com\nResolve-DnsName portal.example.com\nTest-NetConnection portal.example.com -Port 443\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>These Windows checks show the configured DNS servers, query a name, and test the service’s TCP port. Replace the example with the actual affected hostname. \u003ccode>Test-NetConnection\u003c/code> proves a TCP connection can be established, not that HTTPS or the application is healthy. Browsing to a bare IP can fail because certificates and website routing expect a hostname.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Read the symptom before changing settings\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Symptom\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Useful next check\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Name does not exist / NXDOMAIN\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Check spelling and whether the expected record exists in the correct DNS zone.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Query times out\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Check reachability to the configured resolver and whether it answers other queries.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>An unexpected address is returned\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Compare intended records, caches, split DNS, and recent changes.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Correct address, but application fails\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Check the service port, TLS, proxy, firewall, and application health.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Internal names may only exist on company DNS servers, often reachable through an approved remote-access path. Replacing corporate DNS with a public resolver can break domain logons or file-server lookups. Record the query, resolver, answer, and time before clearing a cache; that evidence can explain what happened.\u003c/p>","question":"A name resolves to the expected IP, but the website still fails. What has DNS proved?","answer":"It has provided the expected address for that lookup. It has not proved that the service port is reachable, the TLS handshake works, or the application is healthy. Test those separately and compare the scope of affected users.","sources":["dns"],"related":["dhcp","tls"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — Troubleshooting DNS clients","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/dns/troubleshoot/troubleshoot-dns-client"}]},{"slug":"dhcp","title":"DHCP","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"DHCP automatically gives a device an IP address and the network settings it needs. The address is borrowed for a lease period, rather than manually typed into every computer.","body":"\u003ch2>What a new laptop needs\u003c/h2>\u003cp>When a laptop joins an office network, it needs an available IP address, a subnet mask, a default gateway for reaching other networks, and DNS servers for looking up names. \u003cstrong>Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol\u003c/strong> supplies these settings. A home router often runs the DHCP service, but a business may use Windows Server, a firewall, or another network service. DHCP is not limited to routers.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>The first lease: DORA\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Discover:\u003c/strong> the client looks for a DHCP server. It does not yet have a usable lease for this network.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Offer:\u003c/strong> a server offers an address and configuration.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Request:\u003c/strong> the client requests the selected offer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Acknowledge:\u003c/strong> the server confirms the lease. The client can apply the settings after its normal checks.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"dhcp\" aria-label=\"Watch a laptop join the network\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>Watch a laptop join the network\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Step through the initial IPv4 lease exchange, then try it with the server unavailable.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>Enable JavaScript to use this exercise. The worked examples in this note cover the same ideas.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\u003cp>This is the common initial IPv4 exchange. Renewing an existing lease usually starts with a Request, not a complete Discover/Offer cycle. DHCPv4 uses UDP ports 67 for the server and 68 for the client.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Read a DHCP scope\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Setting\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Example and purpose\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Scope\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The address pool and settings for one subnet: 10.20.30.0/24.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Pool\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Offer 10.20.30.50–10.20.30.200 to clients.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Exclusion\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Keep statically configured devices out of the offered pool.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reservation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Give a particular client a consistent address through DHCP, commonly identified by its MAC address or client identifier.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Lease\u003c/td>\u003ctd>How long the client may use the assignment before it expires unless renewed.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Options\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Supply the gateway and DNS servers. Option 003 is routers; 006 is DNS servers in common Windows DHCP administration.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A reservation still depends on DHCP. A manually configured static address is set on the device itself. Plan both so two devices are not assigned the same address.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Why VLANs sometimes need a relay\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The first broadcast does not normally cross a router. If the DHCP server is on another subnet, a \u003cstrong>DHCP relay\u003c/strong> forwards the request and identifies the client’s network so the server can choose the right scope. A new VLAN may have perfectly good switch ports but no functioning DHCP path if its relay or scope is missing.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Troubleshoot “I have no network”\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Start with \u003ccode>ipconfig /all\u003c/code>. Check whether DHCP is enabled, which server supplied the lease, the address, mask, gateway, DNS servers, and lease times. On a Windows client configured for DHCP, an unexpected \u003ccode>169.254.x.x\u003c/code> link-local address is a clue that it could not obtain a lease. It is not proof of the exact failure.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Compare another device on the same VLAN. Check scope exhaustion, server health, relay settings, switch VLAN membership, and unexpected DHCP servers. A wrong DNS option can produce a valid IP but broken name lookups. Releasing an address can disconnect a remote-support session, so plan any renewal work around the user’s access.\u003c/p>","question":"Devices on one new VLAN get 169.254 addresses, while other VLANs work. What would you check?","answer":"Confirm the clients are using DHCP and are on the intended VLAN. Check that VLAN’s scope, available addresses, gateway relay, and path to the DHCP server. A failure limited to one VLAN points toward its configuration or path, though the address alone does not prove the cause.","sources":["dhcp","dhcpScope","dhcpRFC"],"related":["dns","vlans"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — DHCP Server overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/dhcp/dhcp-top"},{"title":"Microsoft — DHCP scopes","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/dhcp/dhcp-scopes"},{"title":"RFC 2131 — DHCP messages and leases","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2131"}]},{"slug":"syslog","title":"Syslog","category":"Cloud & telemetry","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"Syslog is a common way for devices and applications to send event messages to a central place. Instead of opening every firewall or server, an analyst can review their messages together.","body":"\u003ch2>Start with one event\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A switch port goes down. A firewall blocks a connection. A Linux service fails to start. Each system can write a message describing what happened. With syslog forwarding configured, it also sends that message to a \u003cstrong>collector\u003c/strong>: a server that receives logs from other systems.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>log\u003c/strong> is a recorded event. A collector stores or forwards those events. A \u003ca href=\"kb-siem.html\">SIEM\u003c/a> can search and analyze collected events and create alerts using rules. Receiving a syslog message does not automatically mean an attack was detected.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Read the message in pieces\u003c/h2>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>&lt;134&gt;1 2026-09-05T10:15:00Z fw-01 firewall 812 DENY - Blocked TCP from 10.20.10.25 to 10.20.30.10:445\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>This fictional RFC 5424-style message records one blocked connection. \u003ccode>fw-01\u003c/code> identifies the reporting device; the timestamp says when it recorded the event. The final text explains the action, source, and destination. \u003ccode>445\u003c/code> is a service port commonly used for SMB file sharing.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Field\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">How to read it\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>&lt;134&gt;\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Priority combines a facility and severity. Here 16 × 8 + 6 = 134: local0 facility, informational severity.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The syslog message format version.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2026-09-05T10:15:00Z\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Event time; Z means UTC.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>fw-01 / firewall / 812\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hostname, application name, and process identifier.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>DENY / -\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Message identifier, then no structured data.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Blocked TCP…\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The human-readable event description. Vendor message bodies vary.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"syslog\" aria-label=\"Which messages reach the collector?\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>Which messages reach the collector?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Change the forwarding threshold. Lower severity numbers are more urgent; the word “blocked” does not automatically mean “critical.”\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>Enable JavaScript to use this exercise. The worked examples in this note cover the same ideas.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>Severity is a label, not a verdict\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Syslog has eight severity levels: 0 emergency, 1 alert, 2 critical, 3 error, 4 warning, 5 notice, 6 informational, and 7 debug. The sender assigns a level. A normal firewall deny can be informational. An informational event can still matter to an investigation. If a device forwards only warning and more urgent messages, levels 5–7 may never reach the collector.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>How the messages travel\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The device needs a destination collector address, a transport and port, and a choice of messages to forward. Traditional UDP syslog commonly uses port 514. UDP provides no delivery acknowledgement, so messages can be lost. Syslog over TLS commonly uses TCP 6514 to protect the connection. Plain TCP alone does not encrypt the messages; the sender and receiver must support and agree on the chosen transport.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Use it in an investigation\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Suppose a user cannot reach a file server. Search the firewall’s collected events for the user’s source IP, the server’s destination IP, port 445, and the time of the attempt. A matching deny can identify the rule that blocked it. Record the rule and intended access before changing it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>If no message appears, check whether the device generated it locally, whether its forwarding filter includes that severity, and whether the collector is receiving other events. Then check parsing, timestamps, and the search time range. “No results” can mean missing collection or a wrong query, not “nothing happened.”\u003c/p>","question":"A firewall shows a local informational deny event, but the collector only receives warning and more urgent events. Why is your search empty?","answer":"Informational is severity 6; warning is 4. A threshold that includes only 0–4 excludes that deny. Check the forwarding filter and collection path before concluding that the event did not happen. The sender’s severity is not an analyst’s final threat assessment.","sources":["syslog","syslogUDP","syslogTLS"],"related":["siem"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"RFC 5424 — Syslog","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5424"},{"title":"RFC 5426 — Syslog over UDP","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5426"},{"title":"RFC 5425 — Syslog over TLS","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5425"}]},{"slug":"mdr","title":"EDR, MDR, and XDR","category":"Security operations","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"EDR watches devices. XDR brings signals from several security areas together. MDR provides people and processes to investigate and respond using those tools.","body":"\u003ch2>Start with the problem\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A user opens an attachment and something suspicious runs on their laptop. You need to know what ran, whether it caused harm, whether other accounts or devices are involved, and who will respond. EDR, XDR, and MDR address different parts of this work. They are not three mutually exclusive products or a required sequence of upgrades.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Understand each role\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Term\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What it contributes\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Example\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Software records device activity such as processes, files, and network connections. It can detect threats and perform supported response actions.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>SentinelOne or Microsoft Defender for Endpoint.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Extended Detection and Response (XDR)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A platform links related signals across areas such as endpoints, email, identities, and cloud applications.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Connecting a phishing email, a suspicious process, and a risky account sign-in into an investigation.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Managed Detection and Response (MDR)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A service supplies analysts and an agreed process for monitoring, investigation, and response. It uses its own and/or integrated tools.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A Blackpoint SOC analyst investigates an integrated SentinelOne alert and takes action within the contracted scope.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>endpoint\u003c/strong> is a device such as a laptop or server. \u003cstrong>Telemetry\u003c/strong> means the recorded activity it sends. A \u003cstrong>SOC\u003c/strong>, or security operations center, is the team and function that monitors and responds to threats. MDR adds that operational service; XDR describes a technology approach, not automatically a staffed team.\u003c/p>\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"response\" aria-label=\"Follow one suspicious attachment\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>Follow one suspicious attachment\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Step through the same fictional incident to see what the endpoint tool, broader platform, and responding people contribute.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>Enable JavaScript to use this exercise. The worked examples in this note cover the same ideas.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>How an EDR can feed an MDR service\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An endpoint agent sends activity and alerts to its product’s platform. A supported integration can share those alerts and relevant context with an MDR provider, often through an API: a structured connection between products. The provider’s analysts investigate with the available evidence and their own sensors or connected services. Depending on permissions, they may use that integration to stop a process or isolate a device.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For a concrete example, Blackpoint documents a SentinelOne integration that monitors alerts, supports response, and writes resolution information back into SentinelOne. This requires a configured integration and service coverage. It is not a guarantee that every raw endpoint event or firewall log is included in every MDR plan.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Where XDR and a SIEM fit\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An XDR platform can connect the endpoint alert to an email delivered to the same person and a suspicious sign-in. A \u003ca href=\"kb-siem.html\">SIEM\u003c/a> provides broader log collection, search, and detection across configured sources. Their capabilities can overlap and integrate. Microsoft Sentinel is a separate product from SentinelOne; Sentinel and Defender XDR can share an investigation experience.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>What an analyst still needs to do\u003c/h2>\u003cul>\u003cli>Confirm the customer, affected device, user, timestamp, and evidence behind the alert.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Read what automatic response or the MDR provider already completed. “Detected” is different from “successfully contained.”\u003c/li>\u003cli>Determine scope: other devices, mailbox activity, account use, and business impact.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Follow the response plan. Urgent containment can happen while investigation continues when the evidence and authority justify it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Document actions, owners, outstanding tasks, and the evidence needed before restoring access.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003caside class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>Do not equate the labels\u003c/strong>\u003cp>MDR does not simply mean “a place to view firewall logs.” The defining feature is the managed investigation and response service. Verify the actual data sources and responsibilities in the deployed service.\u003c/p>\u003c/aside>","question":"Why would an organization use SentinelOne and Blackpoint together instead of choosing only one?","answer":"SentinelOne supplies endpoint visibility and response controls. With the supported integration and coverage configured, Blackpoint can provide continuous monitoring, investigation, and response around those alerts and its other evidence. The organization still coordinates business impact, remediation, and follow-up. XDR can add broader correlation across security areas.","sources":["edr","xdr","blackpointS1","mdrEDR"],"related":[],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"SentinelOne — Endpoint protection","url":"https://www.sentinelone.com/platform/endpoint-protection-platform/"},{"title":"Microsoft — Defender XDR overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-xdr/microsoft-365-defender"},{"title":"Blackpoint — SentinelOne integration","url":"https://blackpointcyber.com/integrations/sentinelone/"},{"title":"Blackpoint — Managed EDR","url":"https://blackpointcyber.com/why-blackpoint/managed-edr/"}]},{"slug":"siem","title":"SIEM","category":"Security operations","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"A SIEM collects searchable security data from different systems and applies detection rules. It helps an analyst investigate a sequence of events across the environment.","body":"\u003ch2>Why collect logs together?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A firewall can show a blocked connection. A Windows server can show a failed logon. An email system can show message delivery. Opening each device separately is slow, especially across many customers. A \u003cstrong>Security Information and Event Management\u003c/strong> platform brings configured sources into a place where you can search and compare them. Microsoft Sentinel is one example.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Follow the data\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Generate:\u003c/strong> a source system records an event.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Collect:\u003c/strong> an agent, API connector, or log forwarder sends it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Parse:\u003c/strong> fields such as timestamp, account, IP, and action become searchable. Some connectors also normalize different vendor formats into common fields.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Detect:\u003c/strong> a rule evaluates the data, for example many failed logons followed by a success.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Investigate:\u003c/strong> an analyst examines the alert and related evidence. Several related alerts may be grouped into an incident.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>event\u003c/strong> records something that happened. An \u003cstrong>alert\u003c/strong> flags activity that matched detection logic. An \u003cstrong>incident\u003c/strong> is a unit of investigation that can group related alerts and entities. These are not interchangeable.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Work through a simple case\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Imagine 30 failed logons against one server, followed by a successful logon from the same source. A detection rule might raise an alert. The analyst asks whether the account normally signs in that way, what kind of logon succeeded, and what happened afterward. The pattern could indicate password guessing, but it could also involve a broken service followed by a legitimate user. The raw events and context support the conclusion.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A rule must define its time window, grouping, exclusions, and threshold. Thirty failures against one user are a different question from thirty failures spread across many users. Tuning is the process of adjusting detection logic to make it useful while preserving visibility.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>What a dashboard cannot prove\u003c/h2>\u003cul>\u003cli>No alert does not prove no attack occurred. Data may be missing, a rule may not cover the behavior, or a threshold may not be reached.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A map based on an IP address estimates the address’s location. It does not identify the attacker’s physical location.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A colorful dashboard is a view of data. Verify source health, timestamps, and fields before trusting its interpretation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Central collection does not automatically grant response authority. Automated actions need configured permissions and an approved workflow.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch2>Use it during daily review\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Check the incident queue and source health. For an investigation, select the correct customer or workspace, define a time range, and search the account, device, or IP. Record relevant results and the query that produced them. Compare endpoint and identity evidence where available. Escalate with a concise timeline, impact, actions already taken, and the next decision needed.\u003c/p>","question":"How can a SIEM contain logs without producing an alert about those logs?","answer":"Collection and detection are separate. The source may be sending events successfully while no enabled rule matches the behavior, its threshold is not reached, or its query looks in the wrong table or time range. Check the raw events before changing the detection.","sources":["sentinelOverview","sentinelIncident","ama"],"related":["syslog"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — What is Microsoft Sentinel?","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/sentinel/overview"},{"title":"Microsoft — Investigating a Sentinel incident","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/sentinel/incident-investigation"},{"title":"Microsoft — Windows events via AMA","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/sentinel/connect-services-windows-based"}]},{"slug":"ztna-sase","title":"ZTNA / SASE","category":"Identity & access","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"ZTNA checks who is requesting access and the conditions of that request before allowing a path to a resource. SASE combines secure access with wider networking and security services.","body":"\u003ch2>Start with a remote employee\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An employee at home needs a company finance application. That application might be on a private office server, so an ordinary internet connection is not enough. \u003cstrong>Zero Trust Network Access\u003c/strong> provides an approved path based on identity, the requested resource, and configured context such as device status.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A resource can be an internal web application or another supported private service. The employee may use a local client or a browser, depending on the product and application. They do not necessarily open a virtual Windows desktop. The goal is to grant the required access with the conditions the organization has chosen.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Walk through an access request\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>The employee signs in using the organization’s identity provider.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The service evaluates the applicable access policy: user or group, resource, and relevant device or risk conditions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If requirements are met, the system provides the permitted connection to that resource.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The application still checks its own sign-in and permissions. Network access does not automatically grant access to every file.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Access can be reevaluated when supported signals or sessions change. The exact timing and behavior depend on the implementation.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch2>Compare the experiences\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Approach\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What the employee experiences\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>VPN\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A tunnel provides access to configured network routes. Its reach can be broad or tightly restricted; a VPN does not inherently grant every permission.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>ZTNA\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Access is granted according to identity, resource, and policy context. Supported protocols and the granularity of controls depend on the product.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Virtual desktop, such as a Citrix desktop\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The employee operates a remote desktop or published application. Much of the work runs on the remote system rather than directly on the local laptop.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A personal computer is not universally allowed or forbidden by ZTNA. The organization may require a managed device, allow limited browser access, or use another design. Likewise, “always on” describes how a configured client maintains protection; it does not mean every resource is available to every signed-in user.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Where SASE fits\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Secure Access Service Edge\u003c/strong>, commonly pronounced “sassy,” is the broader architecture. It combines networking, such as software-defined wide area networking (SD-WAN), with cloud-delivered security. Common parts include ZTNA, web filtering through a secure web gateway, controls for cloud application use through a CASB, and firewall-as-a-service. A product may deliver a subset; verify the deployed capabilities.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Use it on a support ticket\u003c/h2>\u003cp>For “I can sign in but cannot reach the app,” separate identity, policy, network path, and application permissions. A successful MFA challenge confirms one requirement, not every requirement. Check the request’s policy result, device state, DNS, connector or gateway health, and the resource itself. A precise denial reason is more useful than turning off a policy to make the error disappear.\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>Conditional Access is one part of the picture\u003c/strong>\u003cp>A sign-in policy can require MFA or a compliant device for an application. By itself, that policy does not create a network path from a home laptop to a private file server. The access service and the application’s own permissions still matter.\u003c/p>\u003c/aside>","question":"Why does successful MFA not guarantee that an employee can open a private file share?","answer":"MFA satisfies an authentication requirement. The device and request must also meet the access policy, the private server must be reachable through an approved path, and the user must have permission on the share and files.","sources":["sase","privateAccess","ca"],"related":[],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Cisco — SASE and its components","url":"https://www.cisco.com/site/us/en/learn/topics/security/what-is-secure-access-service-edge-sase.html"},{"title":"Microsoft — Entra Private Access","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/global-secure-access/concept-private-access"},{"title":"Microsoft — Conditional Access","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/conditional-access/overview"}]},{"slug":"email-auth","title":"Email authentication","category":"Security operations","kind":"Concept","basis":"Scenario study","principle":"SPF, DKIM, and DMARC help check whether a message is authorized to use a domain. They do not prove that the sender is honest or that the request is safe.","body":"\u003ch2>Start with the visible sender\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An email contains several sender-related fields. The display name is the friendly label, such as “Finance Director.” The \u003cstrong>From\u003c/strong> address is what the mail client displays. The \u003cstrong>envelope sender\u003c/strong> is used during delivery and can differ from that visible From address. Authentication checks these identities in specific ways; a convincing display name proves very little.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>What each check means\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Check\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Plain-English question\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Limit\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>SPF — Sender Policy Framework\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Is this sending server authorized in DNS to send for the envelope-sender domain?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>An SPF pass alone does not authenticate the visible From domain. Forwarding can complicate SPF.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>DKIM — DomainKeys Identified Mail\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Does a valid signature from the signing domain verify the signed parts of this message?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>It checks signed content and domain responsibility, not the truth of the message or every possible header.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>DMARC — Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting, and Conformance\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Did SPF or DKIM pass with a domain that aligns with the visible From domain?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A passing message can still be fraudulent. A failing result needs delivery-path context.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>DMARC needs at least one aligned passing method, not both. \u003cstrong>Alignment\u003c/strong> means the authenticated domain matches the visible From domain according to the configured strict or relaxed rules. Relaxed alignment can allow related subdomains of the same organizational domain.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Compare three messages\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Fictional case\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What the checks can tell you\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Forged From: finance@company.example, sent through an unauthorized system\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DMARC can fail because neither SPF nor DKIM passes with alignment to company.example.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>From: finance@company-payments.example, a lookalike domain owned by an attacker\u003c/td>\u003ctd>SPF, DKIM, and DMARC can all pass for the attacker’s own domain. The domain still is not company.example.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>A real employee mailbox has been compromised\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Messages sent through the legitimate service can authenticate successfully while carrying fraudulent instructions.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch2>Read it during phishing review\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Use your email security platform or the original message headers to inspect the receiving system’s authentication results. Compare the actual From address, Reply-To, envelope sender, DKIM signing domain, and links with the organization the sender claims to represent. A raw authentication-looking header supplied by an untrusted sender is not automatically trustworthy; use the results from your trusted receiving system.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Then inspect the request itself. Was a bank account changed? Is the message pressuring the user to bypass a normal process? Did the recipient click, download, enter credentials, or approve MFA? Search for other recipients and related activity through the available email tools.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Understand the policy\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A domain’s DMARC policy can request monitoring only (\u003ccode>p=none\u003c/code>), quarantine, or rejection of failing mail. Receiver handling also depends on the receiving system’s policies. Publishing a monitoring policy is not the same as enforcing rejection.\u003c/p>","question":"Why can a lookalike domain pass DMARC?","answer":"The attacker can configure valid SPF and DKIM for the domain they own, aligned with that domain’s From address. DMARC checks domain authorization, not whether the domain is the real company or whether the payment request is legitimate.","sources":["spf","dkim","dmarc"],"related":["bec","dns"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — SPF email authentication","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-office-365/email-authentication-spf-configure"},{"title":"Microsoft — DKIM email authentication","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-office-365/email-authentication-dkim-configure"},{"title":"Microsoft — DMARC and alignment","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-office-365/email-authentication-dmarc-configure"}]},{"slug":"bec","title":"Business email compromise","category":"Security operations","kind":"Concept","basis":"Scenario study","principle":"Business email compromise is a scam that uses a believable business conversation to trick someone into sending money, sharing information, or changing an important process.","body":"\u003ch2>Recognize the attack\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A message appears to come from a supplier: “Our bank details changed; please use this account.” It may contain no malware and no suspicious attachment. The attacker wants the employee to perform the harmful action willingly. BEC is an attack scenario, not an email security framework.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The message might use a forged sender, a similar-looking domain, or an actual compromised mailbox. Attackers can reply inside an existing conversation and copy its tone. A familiar name, a real signature, and passing email authentication do not establish that a new payment instruction is legitimate.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Start with the requested action and exposure\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>Ask the recipient to pause the requested action and avoid further interaction with the suspicious message.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Determine what already happened: reply, click, download, credential entry, MFA approval, data disclosure, or payment.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Preserve the original message and useful identifiers, headers, timestamps, and relevant conversation history.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Verify the business request through a known independent contact method, such as a number already in approved supplier records. Do not use new contact details supplied by the suspect message.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch2>Investigate more than the header\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Inspect the sender domains, Reply-To, authentication results, links, and message delivery. Search for other recipients and related messages. If a mailbox may be compromised, examine sign-ins, mailbox rules, forwarding, sent messages, permission changes, and other relevant audit activity with the tools available. Distinguish what is observed from what is still suspected.\u003c/p>\u003cp>SPF, DKIM, and DMARC help evaluate domain use. They do not clear a legitimate but compromised account or a well-configured lookalike domain. The authenticity of a payment change still needs independent business verification.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Match the response to what happened\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Exposure\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Typical response work within the incident plan\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Message received, no interaction\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Evaluate and scope the message; remove or quarantine confirmed malicious copies as authorized and explain the finding to the user.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Credentials entered or suspicious MFA approved\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Coordinate account containment, credential and session response, authentication-method review, and investigation of account activity.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Attachment executed\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Investigate the endpoint and consider immediate containment using the available evidence and authority.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Payment or sensitive data already sent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Escalate immediately through the organization’s incident and business response process. For payment fraud, the responsible team should promptly contact its financial institution.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch2>Make the handoff useful\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Record the claimed sender, actual domain, recipients, actions the user took, evidence, affected accounts or devices, containment already completed, and the owner of each next step. A high-impact event may need containment and escalation while further confirmation continues. Avoid a rule such as “always wait until completely certain” that could delay an urgent response.\u003c/p>","question":"A supplier’s email passes DMARC and asks for new bank details. Is it safe to approve the change?","answer":"No. A compromised supplier mailbox or a lookalike domain can pass authentication. Pause the change, verify it through an existing trusted contact method, and investigate the message and any user interaction. Escalate promptly if money or sensitive information has already been sent.","sources":["bec","dmarc","compromised"],"related":["email-auth","identity"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"FBI — Business email compromise","url":"https://www.fbi.gov/how-we-can-help-you/common-frauds-and-scams/business-email-compromise"},{"title":"Microsoft — DMARC and alignment","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-office-365/email-authentication-dmarc-configure"},{"title":"Microsoft — Respond to a compromised email account","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-office-365/responding-to-a-compromised-email-account"}]},{"slug":"identity","title":"Risky sign-ins","category":"Identity & access","kind":"Concept","basis":"Scenario study","principle":"A risky sign-in is an authentication event with signals that deserve investigation. The signal helps prioritize work; the surrounding evidence determines whether an account is compromised.","body":"\u003ch2>Understand the signal\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An account signs in from Colorado and soon afterward appears to sign in from another continent. That travel seems implausible, so an identity system may flag it. An \u003cstrong>impossible-travel\u003c/strong> style detection compares time and apparent locations; other signals include unfamiliar properties or known malicious infrastructure. Names and logic vary by provider.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A network location is normally inferred from the IP address. A VPN, mobile provider, cloud proxy, or inaccurate location database can change that appearance without the user physically moving. Conversely, a familiar location is not proof of safety. Attackers may use nearby infrastructure or an already-established session.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Read the sign-in as a record\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Field\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Why it matters\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>User and tenant\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Identify the actual account and organization. Similar display names can mislead.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Time and result\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Establish whether authentication succeeded and keep the time zone consistent.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Application / resource\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Identify what the account was trying to access.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>IP, device, client, and location\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Compare with the account’s usual pattern and known company infrastructure.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Authentication details\u003c/td>\u003ctd>See how MFA or another requirement was satisfied; a success can rely on an earlier claim or session.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Conditional Access result\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Identify which policies applied, which requirement failed, or why access was allowed.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch2>Work through the investigation\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>Confirm the alert’s evidence and whether suspicious access succeeded. Compare nearby sign-ins, devices, and expected VPN use.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Contact the user through a trusted channel to verify relevant activity, without relying only on their recollection.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Look for subsequent resource activity: mailbox rules, sent messages, downloaded files, changed authentication methods, or new permissions where logs are available.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Assess impact and urgency. Use the incident plan to contain credible active compromise while continuing investigation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Document the evidence, alternative explanations, actions, and the remaining uncertainty.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch2>Containment is more than a password reset\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Authorized response can include blocking sign-in, resetting credentials, revoking sessions, and reviewing registered authentication methods or suspicious application access. These actions have different effects. Revocation is not a promise that every existing token or application session immediately becomes unusable; token lifetime, application behavior, and supported continuous evaluation matter. Verify the observed outcome.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Do not delete every authentication method automatically because a location looked unusual. Determine what was changed or abused and use the approved recovery procedure. Similarly, changing a password does not remove a malicious forwarding rule or identify which data was accessed.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Use the right source of evidence\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Entra sign-in logs explain authentication. Email and cloud application audit records show relevant activity within those services. Endpoint tools show device activity. Intune provides device-management and compliance information; it is not a universal record of everything an attacker did.\u003c/p>","question":"Two successful sign-ins appear far apart within minutes. What would make you more or less concerned?","answer":"Compare known VPN or proxy use, device and client details, authentication and policy results, and subsequent activity. A user-confirmed VPN with expected behavior may explain the location shift. Unexpected MFA changes, mailbox forwarding, or unfamiliar resource access increase concern and may justify prompt containment under the response plan.","sources":["risk","ca","compromised"],"related":["bec"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — Identity risk detections","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/id-protection/concept-identity-protection-risks"},{"title":"Microsoft — Conditional Access","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/conditional-access/overview"},{"title":"Microsoft — Respond to a compromised email account","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-office-365/responding-to-a-compromised-email-account"}]},{"slug":"windows-domain","title":"Active Directory","category":"Identity & access","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"Active Directory Domain Services keeps a shared directory of users, computers, and groups. Domain controllers use it to support domain authentication and centralized administration.","body":"\u003ch2>Server, role, and domain controller\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Windows Server\u003c/strong> is an operating system. \u003cstrong>Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS)\u003c/strong> is a role it can run. A server promoted to run AD DS as a domain controller maintains the directory and provides domain services. A file server can run Windows Server without being a domain controller.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>domain\u003c/strong> is a directory and administration boundary containing objects such as user accounts and computers. A \u003cstrong>group\u003c/strong> collects identities so permissions can be assigned together. An \u003cstrong>organizational unit (OU)\u003c/strong> is a container used to organize objects, delegate administration, and apply Group Policy. An OU is not a permission group.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Follow an ordinary domain sign-in\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>A domain-joined PC has a relationship with the domain. It uses DNS to locate appropriate domain services.\u003c/li>\u003cli>During a typical connected domain sign-in, Windows communicates with a domain controller to authenticate the user. Kerberos is commonly used.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Kerberos provides tickets the user can use to request access to supported services without sending the password to every server.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The destination service checks authorization, including group membership and its own permissions. A successful logon does not grant access to all files.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Windows can permit cached interactive sign-in when a domain controller is unavailable, depending on configuration and prior sign-ins. Reaching the desktop therefore does not prove the PC can currently contact a domain controller or obtain access to a network resource.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>What Group Policy does\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Administrators use Group Policy Objects (GPOs) to apply settings to users and computers, such as password-related configuration, firewall settings, or drive mappings. Where a GPO is linked, its filtering, inheritance, and processing determine who receives it. Changing a domain-wide GPO can affect many systems.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Troubleshoot from the symptom\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Symptom\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Useful checks\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>One user cannot sign in\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Exact error, correct domain, account enabled or locked, password status, and relevant log events.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Many devices cannot use domain services\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DNS configuration, domain controller reachability, service health, and recent changes.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Desktop sign-in works but a share fails\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Network path, current domain connectivity, user identity, group membership, and share/file permissions.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Expected policy is missing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Applied-policy results, object location, links and filtering, and domain connectivity.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch2>Keep AD DS and Entra ID distinct\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Microsoft Entra ID, formerly Azure AD, is Microsoft’s cloud identity service. It is not simply an on-premises domain controller with a new name. Organizations can synchronize selected identities between AD DS and Entra ID, so a user may appear in both. Identify whether the problem concerns a local account, domain account, cloud sign-in, or synchronization before choosing a console or reset procedure.\u003c/p>","question":"Does every Windows Server machine validate domain logons?","answer":"No. Windows Server can host many roles. Domain controllers running AD DS provide domain authentication services. A server used only for file sharing or an application is not automatically a domain controller.","sources":["ad","kerberos","adCompare"],"related":["file-shares"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — Active Directory Domain Services overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/get-started/virtual-dc/active-directory-domain-services-overview"},{"title":"Microsoft — Kerberos authentication","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/security/kerberos/kerberos-authentication-overview"},{"title":"Microsoft — Compare directory services","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/domain-services/compare-identity-solutions"}]},{"slug":"file-shares","title":"File shares","category":"Identity & access","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"A file share lets other computers access a folder over the network. The network path finds the server and share; the user’s permissions decide what they can do there.","body":"\u003ch2>Read the path\u003c/h2>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>\\\\files-01\\Finance\nZ:  →  \\\\files-01\\Finance\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>A path beginning with two backslashes is a \u003cstrong>UNC path\u003c/strong>. \u003ccode>files-01\u003c/code> is the server name and \u003ccode>Finance\u003c/code> is the share name. A mapped drive such as \u003ccode>Z:\u003c/code> is a convenient local shortcut to that path. Losing the drive letter does not necessarily mean the files or server disappeared.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A file server can be a general-purpose server offering folders. A \u003cstrong>NAS\u003c/strong>, or network-attached storage device, is a storage-focused appliance that can also offer shares. Both may provide SMB, the file-sharing protocol commonly used by Windows. Modern direct SMB commonly uses TCP port 445.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Four things must work\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Name:\u003c/strong> the computer resolves the server name to the intended address.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Path:\u003c/strong> the network and any approved remote-access service can reach the server’s SMB service.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Identity:\u003c/strong> the server authenticates the account being used.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Permission:\u003c/strong> the account is authorized to use the share and the requested files.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Being on the same network or connected through a VPN does not expose every person’s files automatically. A folder must be shared and permissions must allow the requested access. The same applies when an access service permits the network connection.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Share permissions and file permissions\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Windows file access over a share is constrained by both the share’s permissions and the file system’s permissions, commonly NTFS permissions. Allowing Modify on the share does not override a file permission that prevents the write. Use effective-access tools and the actual group memberships when troubleshooting; inherited permissions and deny entries can make a quick glance misleading.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Troubleshoot a missing mapped drive\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>Record the drive letter, expected UNC path, user, error, and whether other users are affected.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Try the known UNC path. If it works, investigate the mapping or its deployment policy.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If the name fails, check DNS and the remote-access path. A service-port test can help separate reachability from authorization.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If the share opens but writing fails, check the exact account, share permissions, file permissions, and storage state.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If everyone is affected, inspect server and service health, disk space, and recent changes.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Resolve-DnsName files-01\nTest-NetConnection files-01 -Port 445\nGet-SmbMapping\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>These Windows checks inspect name resolution, a TCP connection, and existing drive mappings. Substitute the actual server. A successful TCP check does not prove the user can authenticate or read a folder. Avoid granting broad access as a shortcut; restore the intended access for the affected group.\u003c/p>","question":"Z: disappeared, but the expected UNC path opens successfully. What is your next focus?","answer":"The server path and this user’s basic access work. Investigate the drive mapping, sign-in script, Group Policy, or device-management configuration that should create Z:. Also check whether the problem is limited to one session or device.","sources":["smb","dns","kerberos"],"related":["windows-domain"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — SMB file sharing","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/storage/file-server/file-server-smb-overview"},{"title":"Microsoft — Troubleshooting DNS clients","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/dns/troubleshoot/troubleshoot-dns-client"},{"title":"Microsoft — Kerberos authentication","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/security/kerberos/kerberos-authentication-overview"}]},{"slug":"windows-events","title":"Windows events","category":"Cloud & telemetry","kind":"Concept","basis":"Lab application","principle":"Windows event logs record activity on a computer. Event IDs tell you what kind of event was recorded; the event’s fields explain who, where, when, and how.","body":"\u003ch2>Find the event at its source\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Open Event Viewer on the intended Windows machine and inspect the relevant log. \u003cstrong>System\u003c/strong> includes operating-system and service events. \u003cstrong>Application\u003c/strong> contains application events. \u003cstrong>Security\u003c/strong> contains audited security activity. Which events appear depends on audit configuration, system role, activity, and log retention.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Filter by a sensible time range and event ID, then open the event’s details. Note the computer name and time zone. A log collector provides another copy or view of selected data; finding an event locally helps determine whether an apparent gap is a collection problem.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Three sign-in events to recognize\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Event ID\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What it records\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What it does not prove\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>4624\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A successful logon on the computer recording it.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>That a person opened a desktop, or that the access was authorized.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>4625\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A failed logon attempt.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>That an attacker succeeded or that every failure was malicious.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>4672\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sensitive privileges assigned to a new logon.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A fresh privilege-escalation attack; legitimate administrative and system logons can generate it.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch2>Read the logon type\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A logon is not always someone typing into the Windows sign-in screen. \u003cstrong>Type 2\u003c/strong> is interactive; \u003cstrong>type 3\u003c/strong> is network, such as access to a shared resource; \u003cstrong>type 5\u003c/strong> is a service; \u003cstrong>type 10\u003c/strong> is RemoteInteractive, commonly RDP. There are additional types, so check the event reference for unfamiliar values.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A type 3 logon does not mean an RDP desktop session. Also, the “Subject” account can be the account reporting or requesting the operation. Read the relevant New Logon or Account For Which Logon Failed fields rather than treating every account field as the same identity.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Build a small timeline\u003c/h2>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Fictional example on SRV-01\n10:01  4625  user: alex  source: 10.20.10.25\n10:02  4625  user: alex  source: 10.20.10.25\n10:03  4624  user: alex  logon type: 10  logon ID: 0x8F12\n10:03  4672  subject: alex  logon ID: 0x8F12\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>This is a sequence worth checking, but two failed passwords followed by a legitimate admin logon is one possible explanation. Match the computer and Logon ID to associate relevant events within that host session, alongside timestamps and account details. Logon IDs are not a global identifier across every machine or reboot. Then inspect what the session did and whether the source and account use were expected.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Use the fields to avoid overclaiming\u003c/h2>\u003cp>For failed logons, inspect the status and substatus, logon type, account, and source information. For successes, determine the session type and related activity. A missing or placeholder IP does not identify a remote source. Record “successful authentication observed” separately from “compromise confirmed.”\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>Local first, then collection\u003c/strong>\u003cp>If Event Viewer contains a known event but the query platform does not, check collection rules, agent health, table, workspace, and time range. If neither contains it, check whether that event was generated and auditing was enabled.\u003c/p>\u003c/aside>","question":"A 4624 is followed by a 4672 with the same Logon ID on one host. Does that prove privilege escalation?","answer":"No. It shows a successful logon and sensitive privileges assigned to that session. This can be normal for an administrative or system account. Check the account, logon type, source, time, expected role, and subsequent actions to decide whether the activity is suspicious.","sources":["e4624","e4625","e4672","eventViewer"],"related":["windows-domain"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — Successful logon: 4624","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/auditing/event-4624"},{"title":"Microsoft — Failed logon: 4625","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/auditing/event-4625"},{"title":"Microsoft — Special privileges: 4672","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/auditing/event-4672"},{"title":"Microsoft — Get-WinEvent","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.diagnostics/get-winevent"}]},{"slug":"tls","title":"TLS","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"TLS protects data in transit by encrypting it and checking that it has not been changed. In a normal HTTPS connection, it also helps the browser verify the server’s identity.","body":"\u003ch2>Put it in a web connection\u003c/h2>\u003cp>When you use \u003ccode>https://\u003c/code>, the browser uses HTTP with TLS protection. \u003cstrong>HTTP\u003c/strong> describes the web request and response. \u003cstrong>TLS\u003c/strong>, Transport Layer Security, protects the exchanged data. \u003cstrong>TCP\u003c/strong> commonly provides reliable delivery for HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2, while IP carries packets between network addresses. HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP and incorporates TLS 1.3.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Plain HTTP commonly uses TCP too. TCP gives ordered delivery and recovery from loss; it does not encrypt the application’s contents. Adding encryption is not the same as changing whether traffic uses IP.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>What happens during the handshake?\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>The client and server agree on supported TLS parameters.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The server presents a certificate. The client checks the requested hostname, validity, and trust chain under its validation rules.\u003c/li>\u003cli>They establish shared session keys using the negotiated handshake.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Application data then travels in protected records that provide confidentiality and integrity.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>A certificate links an identity, commonly a hostname, to a public key through a trust system. It is not a promise that the business is honest. A malicious website can have a valid certificate for its own domain.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Keep the security promises precise\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Property\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Meaning\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Confidentiality\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A passive observer should not be able to read protected application contents.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Integrity\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tampering with protected records should be detected.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Authentication\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The client verifies the server’s identity through certificate validation in a typical HTTPS connection. Client certificates are optional in ordinary web use.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Limits\u003c/td>\u003ctd>TLS does not stop a compromised endpoint from reading its own plaintext or make a harmful website safe. Some traffic metadata remains visible.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch2>Versions and certificates\u003c/h2>\u003cp>TLS 1.3 is the modern protocol version. TLS 1.2 can still be secure with appropriate algorithms and configuration; it is not automatically broken just because 1.3 exists. Older SSL and TLS versions should not be used as a workaround. A failed certificate check can indicate an expired certificate, wrong hostname, untrusted issuer, incorrect system time, or authorized or unauthorized interception.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Use it on an application ticket\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A service can answer on TCP 443 while TLS or the application still fails. Record the exact browser or client error and affected hostname. Check the certificate’s subject names, dates, trust chain, and the device’s clock. Compare another device and network path, including any proxy or security inspection. Do not teach the user to ignore certificate warnings as a routine fix.\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>One boundary at a time\u003c/strong>\u003cp>A successful DNS lookup finds the address. A TCP test checks transport reachability. A successful TLS handshake checks the negotiated protected channel. The application still has to respond correctly.\u003c/p>\u003c/aside>","question":"A site has a valid HTTPS certificate. Does that mean the site’s content is trustworthy?","answer":"No. A valid certificate helps authenticate the connection to that hostname and protect data in transit. An attacker can obtain a valid certificate for a domain they control, and a legitimate site can also be compromised.","sources":["tls","tcp","http3"],"related":["tcp","dns"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"RFC 9325 — Secure use of TLS","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9325.html"},{"title":"RFC 9293 — TCP","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9293.html"},{"title":"RFC 9114 — HTTP/3","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114"}]},{"slug":"tcp","title":"TCP","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"TCP carries an ordered stream of bytes between two endpoints. It tracks delivery and retransmits missing data so applications do not have to handle ordinary packet loss themselves.","body":"\u003ch2>Identify the two ends\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A TCP connection has a source IP and port and a destination IP and port. For example, a laptop at \u003ccode>10.0.0.25:53010\u003c/code> might connect to a web service at \u003ccode>192.0.2.80:443\u003c/code>. The client commonly chooses a temporary, or \u003cstrong>ephemeral\u003c/strong>, source port. Replies reverse the source and destination. The documentation address here represents a fictional server.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Two connections to the same server can use different client ports. A browser tab does not map neatly to one TCP connection: requests may share a connection or use several. HTTP/2 can carry multiple request streams over one TCP connection.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Open, transfer, and close\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK:\u003c/strong> the usual three-way handshake establishes a connection and synchronizes initial sequence numbers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Data and acknowledgements:\u003c/strong> the sender transmits bytes and the receiver indicates what it has received.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Retransmission:\u003c/strong> missing data can be sent again. Flow control limits data to what the receiver can handle; congestion control responds to network conditions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>FIN / ACK or RST:\u003c/strong> a connection can close in an orderly way or be reset. Interpret the surrounding packets before deciding why.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch2>Sequence number versus conversation number\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A TCP \u003cstrong>sequence number\u003c/strong> identifies a position in the byte stream. If a segment starts at sequence 1000 and carries 100 data bytes, the next data byte would normally be sequence 1100. An acknowledgement generally identifies the next byte expected. SYN and FIN each consume sequence-number space too.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Wireshark’s \u003ccode>tcp.stream\u003c/code> is different: it is an analysis index identifying a conversation within a capture. It is not transmitted in the TCP header. The stream index helps you select a conversation; sequence numbers help you follow the bytes inside it.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Why the header length is a multiple of four\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A TCP header starts with a fixed 20-byte part. Optional fields can make it longer. Its \u003cstrong>Data Offset\u003c/strong> field stores the total header length in units of four bytes, called 32-bit words. A value of 5 means 20 bytes; 8 means 32 bytes. The receiver uses that length to find where application data begins.\u003c/p>\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"tcp\" aria-label=\"Find where the payload begins\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>Find where the payload begins\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Change the number of option bytes. See how padding makes the total TCP header fit the four-byte units used by Data Offset.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>Enable JavaScript to use this exercise. The worked examples in this note cover the same ideas.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\u003cp>A No-Operation option is one byte and can align options. End-of-option-list padding can also fill remaining header space. The “packing peanuts” analogy is about alignment, not adding application data. For a simple 20-byte base plus 10 bytes of options, two more bytes make a 32-byte header.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Use it during troubleshooting\u003c/h2>\u003cp>No reply to SYN can indicate filtering, routing, an unavailable host, or capture limitations. A reset can indicate refusal or a closed connection, but inspect its source and timing. Repeated retransmissions can suggest loss or missing capture visibility. TCP itself does not provide encryption; TLS supplies protection for many applications.\u003c/p>","question":"A TCP segment starts at sequence 1000 and carries 100 data bytes. Is tcp.stream == 1100 the way to find the next bytes?","answer":"No. Sequence 1100 would describe the next data-byte position in this simplified example. tcp.stream is Wireshark’s separate identifier for the whole connection. Filter the right stream, then inspect sequence and acknowledgement numbers inside it.","sources":["tcp","tcpGuide","wireshark"],"related":["tls"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"RFC 9293 — TCP","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9293.html"},{"title":"Wireshark — TCP analysis","url":"https://www.wireshark.org/docs/wsug_html_chunked/ChAdvTCPAnalysis.html"},{"title":"Wireshark — TCP display fields","url":"https://www.wireshark.org/docs/dfref/t/tcp.html"}]}];
